## 11. Port notes (Fastify → Elysia)

Contract preserved 1:1 so the existing client keeps working: same paths (`/api/*`), methods, request bodies, status codes and response bodies.

| Method | Path | Handler |
| --- | --- | --- |
| POST | `/api/register` | `routes/auth.ts` — scrypt + salt, `SET NX`, 201 username / 409 |
| POST | `/api/login` | Redis attempt limit, scrypt verify, signs session JWT into the cookie |
| POST | `/api/logout` | requires session; clears Android push token; deletes cookie → `'logged_out'` |
| POST | `/api/me` | requires session → `{ username }` |
| POST | `/api/push-token` | requires session; Android only → `{ success }` |
| POST | `/api/add-other` | self 400, unknown 404, duplicate 409, else 200 |
| POST | `/api/remove-other` | missing contact 404, else `{ other, count }` |
| GET | `/api/get-others` | `string[]` |
| POST | `/api/conversations` | DM is idempotent (`dm:lookup`), returns 200 when it already exists, 201 otherwise |
| GET | `/api/conversations` | `Conversation[]` |
| POST | `/api/send-message` | participant check, `LLEN` index, Expo push fan-out |
| GET | `/api/get-messages` | `after`/`before`/`limit` pagination → `{ messages, hasMore, totalCount }` |

Redis key patterns are byte-identical to the previous implementation (`user:`, `ratelimit:login:`, `user:*:pushtoken`, `user:*:contacts`, `user:*:conversations`, `conversation:*`, `conversation:*:messages`, `dm:lookup:*`), so existing accounts, contacts, conversations and history keep working.

Deliberate behaviour changes:
- **Error bodies are now `{ message, code }`.** The client's `ApiErrorResponse` reads `data.message`, but the old `ApiError.toJSON()` nested it under `error`, so error messages never reached the UI.
- **CORS is credentialed and origin-restricted.** The old `cors()` allowed every origin without credentials, which browsers reject for cookie auth. Now mirrors the legacy Fastify rule: `DOMAIN` in production, otherwise localhost/127.0.0.1.
- **Logout cannot revoke a copied cookie** — the session is a signed JWT, exactly like the old signed-cookie sessions, so a stolen cookie stays valid until expiry. Server-side revocation (the `sessions` table in `packages/db`, plan Phase 4) is the fix if that matters.
- **Identity is inconsistent between the two halves of the codebase.** The REST port identifies users by username (as Redis and the client do), while `ws/chat.ts` and `/ws/ticket` treat `user.id` as a SQLite user id. With a REST session, `user.id` is a username, so `canAccessRoom` (which matches `room_members.user_id`) will not resolve. Unifying identities is part of finishing the room migration.

Two Elysia-specific traps found while porting, both now documented in code comments:
- `jwt({ exp })` treats a **number as an absolute epoch timestamp**. `exp: 2592000` silently produced tokens that were already expired (`verify` → `false`, every request 401). It must be a duration string: `exp: '2592000s'`.
- `app.handle(new Request('http://x/...'))` returns 404; the same request against `http://localhost/...` matches. Tests use `localhost`.
